Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 16:48:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] Thylacine CL-7b: an ORC MemoryMapper over the I-42
 dual-mapped code Burrow

InProcessMemoryMapper cannot work on Thylacine, and not for a small reason.
W^X is enforced at page-table granularity with no way to change a mapping's
protections afterwards: there is no mprotect syscall at all, and mmap accepts a
PROT argument only to ignore it. So the upstream shape -- reserve RW, write,
raise to RX -- fails at its last step, and fails late: the reserve succeeds,
the content lands, and only the protect call reports that nothing here will
ever be executable.

I-42 answers with two mappings instead of one mutable one. A code Burrow is one
physical region with an RW writer alias and an RX exec alias at unrelated
virtual addresses; no page is ever writable and executable, so W^X holds
unchanged. Emission goes to the writer, execution to the exec alias, and
SYS_ICACHE_SYNC publishes one to the other.

This fits ORC exactly because ORC already draws the same line. prepare() hands
back working memory that need not be the address code runs at -- SharedMemoryMapper
exists because a JIT writing into another process writes at its own mapping of
shared pages and links against the executor's. The two aliases are that same
split with both halves in one address space, so this is SharedMemoryMapper's
addressing over InProcessMemoryMapper's bookkeeping. The delta is four methods:
reserve is SYS_JIT_CREATE, prepare adds the writer/exec displacement, initialize
zero-fills through the writer and publishes instead of calling mprotect, and
release is SYS_JIT_DESTROY (which names the WRITER alias -- the kernel remembers
the pairing, so half a region cannot be released).

Two things worth knowing for whoever touches this next. Upstream's initialize()
memsets the zero-fill tail at the executor address; here that is the read-only
alias, and it is the one place a faithful copy would fault rather than
misbehave. And a segment asking for Write gets the exec alias's RX like every
other, so JIT'd code writing to its own data section at runtime would fault --
link-time writes are fine because JITLink routes all content through prepare().
Recorded rather than rejected: failing initialize() on a Write group would
refuse allocations that work, and the GOT and stubs llvmpipe emits are written
only during linking.
